                "evidence": "LatticeCache 2.3 Pro costs **$45/month**. That includes 20 GB of storage, 50M reads and 10M writes a month. The **largest single item you can store is 5 MB**.\n\n**Included in the $45/month base price:**\n- 20 GB of replicated NVMe cache storage\n- 50 million read ops per month\n- 10 million write ops per month\n\n**Overage rates once you go past those amounts:**\n- Storage: $1.80 per extra GB-month\n- Reads: $0.12 per extra million ops\n- Writes: $0.60 per extra million ops\n\n**Hard limits in 2.3:**\n- Largest single item (value): **5 MB**\n- Longest key: 1,024 bytes\n- Concurrent client connections: 2,000 per cache namespace\n\n**Source:** the research vault page `wiki/LatticeCache.md`. It summarises LatticeCache's official pricing and limits page, which was updated 2026-11-02 and checked against the vault on 2026-11-04. The saved copy is `raw/2026-11/2026-11-04 latticecache-2-3-pricing-limits`. Pricing pages count as fast-changing and get a 30-day shelf life, so this page is good until about 2026-12-04. I didn't search the web because the vault page is current and answers all three parts of your question.\n\nHow much over $45 you'd pay depends on your usage. If you give me your expected storage and monthly reads and writes, I can work out the total.\n\nSources checked: the vault only, through the jvault MCP. I made no web searches, so nothing was spent."
